/// A String refuses every mutating collection member, and says why.
///
/// Issue #42, the other half.  ALGOL-24.md gives each collection kind an
/// invariant and refuses a method exactly where no meaning would preserve both
/// that invariant and what the call asked for.  A String's invariant is that it
/// cannot be modified in place, so every mutating method is refused -- with
/// 'Strings are immutable.', the sentence that already answers an assignment to
/// a position.
///
/// ⚠️ The first assertion PASSES today, under both processors, and it is here
/// for that reason.  S[0] := 'z' already raises the right sentence, so it pins
/// the wording the other refusals must match.  A fix that invented a second
/// sentence for the method forms would leave the language saying two things
/// about one rule, and this file would still fail while looking as though it
/// nearly passed.  Same shape as CharsBang asserting 'Ready?', which works.
///
/// ⚠️ Both processors refuse these calls today, so this file can look already
/// fixed if you only check the exit code.  Neither refuses for the right
/// reason.  Interpreted: 'Only instances have properties.', which rejects the
/// whole dotted form on a String and answers S.Anything identically.  Compiled:
/// 'Only a List or a Set has ''Add''.', which is a receiver complaint -- it
/// would refuse a String even if Strings were mutable, so it is not the
/// immutability guard, it is the absence of one.  The sentence is the assertion
/// precisely because the outcome is already a refusal.
///
/// ⚠️ Separate from StringCollectionMembers.a24 although both are issue #42.
/// They are different changes: one makes a String REACH the collection members,
/// the other makes it REFUSE a subset of them for a stated reason.  A fix that
/// routed String through the collection path without adding the guard would
/// make that file pass and leave a String mutable -- which is worse than the
/// defect, and which one file could not distinguish.
///
/// Interpreted: fails at the second assertion.
/// Compiled:    fails at the second assertion, with a different sentence.
///
test 'A String Refuses To Be Modified';
begin
    var S : String := 'hello';
    var Caught := '';

    // Already correct under both -- pins the sentence.
    try
        S[0] := 'z';
    except
        on E : String do Caught := E;
    end

    AssertEqual ('Strings are immutable.', Caught);

    Caught := '';
    try
        S.Add ('z');
    except
        on E : String do Caught := E;
    end

    AssertEqual ('Strings are immutable.', Caught);

    Caught := '';
    try
        S.Insert (0, 'z');
    except
        on E : String do Caught := E;
    end

    AssertEqual ('Strings are immutable.', Caught);

    Caught := '';
    try
        S.Clear ();
    except
        on E : String do Caught := E;
    end

    AssertEqual ('Strings are immutable.', Caught);

    // The refusal must not have modified it.
    AssertEqual ('hello', S);
end
